  "reason": "The records establish the exact alloy's FCC/B2 lamellar starting structure and show that phase morphology and interface character can control dislocation transfer, stress concentration, cracking, and ductility. Separate literature also establishes thermally exposed L12-containing high-entropy superalloys and the strengthening role of stable ordered precipitates. Thus an expert could reasonably consider thermal processing to alter ordering and interfaces. However, none of the records reports intermediate-temperature aging of AlCoCrFeNi2.1, an ordering maximum near 600°C, jagged FCC/B2 boundaries, APBE-driven suppression of cross-slip, or the resulting ductile-brittle-ductile sequence. Connecting these elements and selecting the specific 600/750°C treatments would require a substantial new idea."
